45:39.240 --> 45:41.140
If a group of neurons receives a sudden,

45:41.340 --> 45:42.740
unexpected spike of input,

45:42.900 --> 45:44.700
like a loud noise or a flash of light,

45:45.080 --> 45:46.800
the system absorbs the shock,

45:47.480 --> 45:48.560
processes the information,

45:48.760 --> 45:50.740
and quickly settles back into its normal,

45:51.140 --> 45:52.380
stable operating range.

45:52.820 --> 45:56.120
The ongoing rhythms of the brain contain the disturbance.

45:56.120 --> 45:59.040
It's like a high-quality shock absorber on a car.

45:59.300 --> 46:00.440
You hit a massive pothole,

46:00.620 --> 46:01.300
the car jolts,

46:01.500 --> 46:04.080
but the suspension immediately absorbs the kinetic energy

46:04.080 --> 46:05.560
and the chassis settles back down.

46:05.860 --> 46:08.520
The trajectory of the car isn't fundamentally ruined.

46:09.020 --> 46:09.920
Under anesthesia,

46:10.240 --> 46:12.320
the shock absorbers fundamentally fail.

46:12.660 --> 46:14.940
The system loses its dynamical boundaries.

46:15.520 --> 46:18.800
If you introduce a perturbation into an anesthetized brain,

46:19.180 --> 46:20.260
the response is chaotic.

46:20.260 --> 46:22.420
It produces poorly bounded,

46:22.620 --> 46:23.060
unpredictable,

46:23.340 --> 46:26.000
or entirely unrecoverable cascades of activity.

46:26.200 --> 46:27.560
The brain loses the ability

46:27.560 --> 46:29.900
to rapidly regulate its own internal state.

46:30.000 --> 46:30.180
Right.

46:30.440 --> 46:32.620
The immiscibility field loses its structure.

46:32.920 --> 46:34.260
And the text says this destabilization

46:34.800 --> 46:36.440
is paired with a massive change

46:36.440 --> 46:37.920
in the brain's phase topology,

46:38.200 --> 46:40.160
how areas synchronize with each other.

